{% comment %}
  One mega panel, for one top-level menu item.

  This panel exists because a block named its menu item — depth of menu has
  nothing to do with it. That is what lets one item with children be a mega and
  the next be an ordinary dropdown, and what lets an item with NO children open
  a panel made purely of cards.

  The link columns, when there are any, come from the Shopify menu rather than
  from settings: a top-level item's children are the column headings and their
  own children are the links under them. That is the shape merchants already
  build in Online Store > Navigation, and it means adding a category is a menu
  edit rather than a theme edit.

  A top-level item whose children have no children of their own has nothing to
  split into columns, so its children become a single column instead. That is
  the old flat dropdown, rendered inside the mega frame — which is what lets a
  simple menu keep working while a deep one gets the full layout.

  Everything that is not a link — the featured product tiles and the promo card
  — comes from the matching mega_panel block, because none of it exists in a
  Shopify menu. A menu item with no block still gets its columns; it simply gets
  no fourth panel.

  Accepts:
  - link {Object} - the top-level menu link this panel belongs to.
  - block {Object} - the matching mega_panel block, or blank.
  - show_counts {Boolean} - print product counts beside collection links.

  Usage:
  {% render 'CWC_header-mega__panel', link: link, block: block, show_counts: show_counts %}
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  comment
    Does any child have children? That is the difference between a menu deep
    enough to column up and one that is not, and it has to be known before the
    first column is printed rather than discovered halfway through.
  endcomment
  assign has_columns = false
  for child in link.links
    if child.links.size > 0
      assign has_columns = true
      break
    endif
  endfor

  comment
    A panel can be all cards and no links now, since a block alone is enough to
    create one. Without this the single-column fallback below would print the
    item's title over an empty list.
  endcomment
  assign has_links = false
  if link.links.size > 0
    assign has_links = true
  endif

  assign has_featured = false
  if block.settings.featured_product_1 != blank or block.settings.featured_product_2 != blank or block.settings.featured_product_3 != blank
    assign has_featured = true
  endif

  assign has_promo = false
  if block.settings.promo_heading != blank or block.settings.promo_text != blank
    assign has_promo = true
  endif

  comment
    The grid is described by how many panels there actually are, so a menu item
    with no promo does not leave a quarter of the bar empty. The narrow variant
    is the Learn case: fewer columns, centred, and capped well short of the full
    header width.
  endcomment
  assign panel_variant = block.settings.variant | default: 'wide'
-%}

<div
  class="cwc_header__mega cwc_header__mega--{{ panel_variant }}"
  data-cwc-mega
  hidden
>
  <div class="cwc_header__mega-inner">
    {%- if has_links == false -%}
      {%- comment -%} Cards-only panel — nothing to column up. {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- elsif has_columns -%}
      {%- for child in link.links -%}
        {%- liquid
          comment
            The footer link for this column — the "All products →" that closes a
            list off.

            Read by column number rather than matched by a chain of conditions,
            so adding a fourth column to the schema is a schema edit alone. It is
            a block setting rather than a menu entry on purpose: as a menu entry
            it would be one more link in the list, styled like the rest and
            impossible to treat differently, and it would exist for every panel
            that shares the menu instead of only the one that wants it.

            A column past the last numbered slot simply gets none, which is the
            right failure: the list still renders, it just does not close with a
            link.
          endcomment
          assign key = 'col' | append: forloop.index | append: '_link_label'
          assign footer_label = block.settings[key]

          assign key = 'col' | append: forloop.index | append: '_link_url'
          assign footer_url = block.settings[key]
        -%}

        <div class="cwc_header__mega-col">
          <div class="cwc_header__mega-heading">{{ child.title }}</div>

          <ul class="cwc_header__mega-list">
            {%- for grandchild in child.links -%}
              <li>
                <a
                  href="{{ grandchild.url }}"
                  class="cwc_header__mega-link{% if grandchild.active %} cwc_header__mega-link--active{% endif %}"
                >
                  {{ grandchild.title }}
                  {%- if show_counts and grandchild.type == 'collection_link' and grandchild.object != blank -%}
                    <span class="cwc_header__mega-count">{{ grandchild.object.all_products_count }}</span>
                  {%- endif -%}
                </a>
              </li>
            {%- endfor -%}

            {%- if footer_label != blank -%}
              <li>
                <a href="{{ footer_url | default: child.url }}" class="cwc_header__mega-all">
                  {{ footer_label }} <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
                </a>
              </li>
            {%- endif -%}
          </ul>
        </div>
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Two levels only. The item's children are the links themselves, under the
        item's own title, so a shallow menu still reads as a column rather than
        as a heading with nothing beneath it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="cwc_header__mega-col">
        <div class="cwc_header__mega-heading">{{ link.title }}</div>

        <ul class="cwc_header__mega-list">
          {%- for child in link.links -%}
            <li>
              <a
                href="{{ child.url }}"
                class="cwc_header__mega-link{% if child.active %} cwc_header__mega-link--active{% endif %}"
              >
                {{ child.title }}
                {%- if show_counts and child.type == 'collection_link' and child.object != blank -%}
                  <span class="cwc_header__mega-count">{{ child.object.all_products_count }}</span>
                {%- endif -%}
              </a>
            </li>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </ul>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if has_featured -%}
      <div class="cwc_header__mega-col cwc_header__mega-col--featured">
        {% if block.settings.featured_heading != blank %}
          <div class="cwc_header__mega-heading">{{ block.settings.featured_heading }}</div>
        {% endif %}

        {%- for tile_number in (1..3) -%}
          {%- liquid
            assign key = 'featured_product_' | append: tile_number
            assign tile_product = block.settings[key]

            assign key = 'featured_label_' | append: tile_number
            assign tile_label = block.settings[key]
          -%}

          {%- if tile_product != blank -%}
            {%- liquid
              comment
                The label is typed and the price is live, joined here rather than
                typed as one string: a merchant who writes "from $56.21" into the
                label has to come back and edit it every time the product is
                repriced, and will not.
              endcomment
              assign tile_price = ''
              if block.settings.featured_show_price
                if tile_product.price_varies
                  assign tile_price = 'from ' | append: tile_product.price_min | money
                else
                  assign tile_price = tile_product.price | money
                endif
              endif

              assign tile_meta = ''
              if tile_label != blank and tile_price != blank
                assign tile_meta = tile_label | append: ' · ' | append: tile_price
              elsif tile_label != blank
                assign tile_meta = tile_label
              else
                assign tile_meta = tile_price
              endif
            -%}

            <a href="{{ tile_product.url }}" class="cwc_header__mega-tile">
              <span class="cwc_header__mega-tile-media">
                {% if tile_product.featured_image != blank %}
                  <img
                    src="{{ tile_product.featured_image | image_url: width: 140 }}"
                    alt="{{ tile_product.featured_image.alt | default: tile_product.title | escape }}"
                    width="70"
                    height="70"
                    loading="lazy"
                  >
                {% endif %}
              </span>

              <span class="cwc_header__mega-tile-body">
                <span class="cwc_header__mega-tile-title">{{ tile_product.title }}</span>
                {% if tile_meta != blank %}
                  <span class="cwc_header__mega-tile-meta">{{ tile_meta }}</span>
                {% endif %}
              </span>
            </a>
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if has_promo -%}
      <div class="cwc_header__mega-promo">
        <div>
          {% if block.settings.promo_heading != blank %}
            <div class="cwc_header__mega-promo-heading">{{ block.settings.promo_heading }}</div>
          {% endif %}

          {% if block.settings.promo_text != blank %}
            <p class="cwc_header__mega-promo-text">{{ block.settings.promo_text }}</p>
          {% endif %}
        </div>

        {% if block.settings.promo_button_label != blank %}
          <a href="{{ block.settings.promo_button_link | default: '#' }}" class="cwc_header__mega-promo-button">
            {{ block.settings.promo_button_label }}
          </a>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</div>
